# Cooperative context switch demo.
#
# Real limitation this design works around (see SPATIAL_COORDINATOR_SPEC.md
# "Context switch" section): the Glyph ISA has no opcode to read the current
# PC into a register, and JMP/CALL/JZ targets are assemble-time immediates —
# there is no register-indirect jump. So an arbitrary mid-instruction PC
# cannot be saved to memory and later resumed from. What CAN be built with
# CALL/RET as-is: a window as a small stateful "tick" routine. The
# supervisor CALLs its fixed entry point (a real immediate CALL — legal),
# the window reads its own persistent counter from WCB memory, advances it,
# writes it back, and RETs (popping the address CALL auto-pushed). State
# genuinely survives across separate dispatches; only "resume mid-body"
# does not.

:supervisor
LDI r31 250            ; stack pointer for CALL/RET (image address space)
LDI r5 100              ; WCB base address (cpu.memory)
LDI r6 8
ADD r6 r5                ; r6 = counter address = WCB base + 8

LDI r9 0
ST r6 r9                  ; counter starts at 0

CALL :window_tick          ; dispatch 1
LD r1 r6
PRT r1                      ; expect 1

CALL :window_tick          ; dispatch 2
LD r1 r6
PRT r1                      ; expect 2

CALL :window_tick          ; dispatch 3
LD r1 r6
PRT r1                      ; expect 3

HALT

:window_tick
LDI r2 100
LDI r3 8
ADD r3 r2                ; r3 = counter address (recomputed independently of supervisor's r6)
LD r4 r3                  ; r4 = current counter
LDI r7 1
ADD r4 r7                  ; increment
ST r3 r4                    ; write back — this is the window's persisted state
RET
